em in threes, with a man hidden under each middle sheep. He himself hangs beneath the finest ram. The blinded giant feels along their backs, never their bellies. Homer even lets Pollifeemus talk to his ram, almost tenderly, as it leaves last. Then Odysseus, safely aboard his ship, cannot resist. His crew begs him to stop. He shouts his real name anyway. Pollifeemus remembers a prophecy that a man named Odysseus would take his sight. He tears the top off a hill and hurls it at the ship, and prays to Poseidon that Odysseus never reaches home. Poseidon listens. For much of the rest of the poem, he keeps Odysseus from home.", "orig": "At dawn, the sheep leave the cave. Odysseus has tied them in threes, with a man hidden under each middle sheep. He himself hangs beneath the finest ram. The blinded giant feels along their backs, never their bellies. Homer even lets Polyphemus talk to his ram, almost tenderly, as it leaves last. Then Odysseus, safely aboard his ship, cannot resist. His crew begs him to stop. He shouts his real name anyway. Polyphemus remembers a prophecy that a man named Odysseus would take his sight. He tears the top off a hill and hurls it at the ship, and prays to Poseidon that Odysseus never reaches home. Poseidon listens. For much of the rest of the poem, he keeps Odysseus from home."}